@startuml
title Catalogue discovery crawl — v4

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
autonumber

control "Queue Consumer" as Consumer
participant "Spotify Web API" as Spotify
database "D1 rebuild tables" as D1

note over Consumer, D1
  Up to eight artists rotate by persisted turn count. Admission favours
  artists missing from the live shelf and already-started discovery;
  completion admits the next artist. After quota waits, queued song/release
  work takes precedence over cold release lists.
  One turn handles one page
  or at most five uncached metadata requests while artists compete.
  Cache hits may fill 30-track batches; the last artist can use a full batch.
  Artist cursors and unfinished ids survive yields and quota waits.
  Identity lasts 30 days; daily album availability is independently refreshed.
  Missing flags or changed returned ids require a full track lookup.
end note

== Preflight (service token) ==

Consumer -> Spotify: Grant SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN\n(PKCE refresh_token + client_id form)
Spotify --> Consumer: Access token + granted scope
Consumer -> D1: Store only encrypted access token until near expiry;\nreuse across later deliveries, renew once on 401
Consumer -> Consumer: Require all five service scopes\n(missing scope → systemic fail)
Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/me
Spotify --> Consumer: Owner profile (immutable account_id,\nmutable user id; country optional)

alt account_id is not the configured OWNER_SPOTIFY_ID
  Consumer -> D1: Mark job failed, outcome "failed"\nlive shelf untouched
else Owner confirmed
  Consumer -> D1: phase=never_identities, capture user id for playlist ownership,\noptional market=owner country; when absent Spotify\napplies the user token country; initial page gap 2s

  == Resolve permanent exact-id Never decisions ==

  loop Up to 30 unresolved exact ids per delivery
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/tracks/{originalNeverId}?market
    Spotify --> Consumer: Full track + external_ids (possibly a\ndifferent market-playable id; no linked_from in Dev Mode)
    Consumer -> D1: Match the requested id to the returned ISRC;\nmark checked for this rebuild atomically
  end
  Consumer -> D1: Retry unresolved 403/404/no-ISRC ids next week;\nphase=seeds only after this gate drains

  == Seed freezing from liked songs ==

  loop Each liked-tracks page (limit 50)
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/me/tracks?limit=50&cursor
    Spotify --> Consumer: Saved-track page + next cursor
    Consumer -> D1: Freeze only the first credited artist\nof each liked track into rebuild_seed_artists;\nalso freeze liked recording keys as known
  end

  == Freeze playlist exclusions before discovery ==

  loop Each owner-playlists page (limit 50)
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/me/playlists?limit=50&cursor
    Spotify --> Consumer: Playlists with owner ids and collaborative flags
    Consumer -> D1: Queue owned and collaborative playlists\nwith snapshot_id; mark verified unchanged versions for reuse
  end

  loop Each queued playlist, page by page
    alt Completed snapshot matches fresh discovery
      Consumer -> D1: Copy cached exact ids and recording exclusions\nwithout reading playlist items
    else Changed or missing snapshot
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/playlists/{id}/items?limit=50&cursor
    Spotify --> Consumer: Playlist items in the current item field
    Consumer -> D1: Record each item's recording key\nin rebuild_known_recordings
    Consumer -> Spotify: After final page, verify snapshot_id
    Consumer -> D1: Publish cache only if version matches;\nthen delete playlist work row
    end
  end

  == Incremental artist discovery ==

  loop Each seed artist (paged, include album + single)
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/artists/{id}/albums\ninclude_groups=album,single&limit=10&market
    alt 200
      Spotify --> Consumer: Album and single releases + cursor
      Consumer -> D1: Queue each release in rebuild_album_queue\nwith the bucket artist id
    else 404 artist vanished
      Spotify --> Consumer: Not found
      Consumer -> D1: Aggregate warning artistsUnavailable;\ndelete completed artist work row
    end

    loop That artist's queued releases
      Consumer -> D1: Try owner/market-scoped discovery page (30 days);\nfetch missing/expired availability pages (1 day) and every new album id
      Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/albums/{id}/tracks?market (market optional)
      Spotify --> Consumer: Simplified tracks
      Consumer -> D1: Filter confirmed known ids, then split remaining ids\ninto enrichment batches of ≤30 in rebuild_enrich_queue
      Consumer -> D1: Delete release work row after its final page
    end

    loop Each enrichment batch (max 30 external reads)
      Consumer -> D1: Read fresh 24h full metadata or combine\n30-day identity with fresh daily availability\n(owner account + market + requested id)
      Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/tracks/{id}\nin groups of at most 5 concurrent requests
      Spotify --> Consumer: Full tracks with external_ids
      Consumer -> D1: Cache successful metadata and checkpoint completed ids\neven if another request fails; retry only unfinished ids
      Consumer -> Consumer: Strict playability gate:\nis_playable=true, not local, complete metadata
      Consumer -> D1: Map to recordings and keep one\nrepresentative per recording (flow 05)
      Consumer -> D1: Atomically publish newly eligible batch recordings\ninto an existing shelf with ledger + slots + counts;\nkeep earlier public representatives unchanged
    end

    Consumer -> D1: If this is the first nonempty shelf,\nregister it live and append dense draw slots;\ncontinue growing it safely
    Consumer -> D1: On legacy artist completion, atomically append\nany remaining eligible staged recordings + ledger;\nkeep existing cards and representatives unchanged
  end

  == Final authoritative liked scan ==

  loop Final authoritative liked scan
    Consumer -> Spotify: GET /v1/me/tracks?limit=50&cursor
    Spotify --> Consumer: Saved-track page
    Consumer -> D1: Record liked recording keys\nin rebuild_known_recordings
  end

  Consumer -> D1: phase=promote (see flow 06)
end

note over Consumer,Spotify #FFF2CC
  Pacing and retries on every Spotify call above:
  · pages start at 2 seconds; 30 track requests wait
    at least 30 seconds. Durable next_read_at and delayed
    queue sends enforce the adaptive request budget.
    Rolling 429s increase pacing and reduce concurrency;
    20 clean units gradually reduce the penalty.
  · rolling 429 → wait exactly Retry-After, no retry
    attempt consumed, work preserved
  · Development Mode QUOTA_EXCEEDED → save progress, honor Retry-After;
    without timing, retry one metadata request after 15m, up to 6h backoff
  · Progress counters commit with cursors; show artists, song checks,
    cache/known skips and recorded refresh requests even while waiting
  · 5xx → persisted exponential backoff (30s, 60s, …),
    after 5 attempts pause 1h, then resume the same work
  · other 4xx → systemic fail
  A systemic fail keeps the live shelf and the failed job
  row intact; the next Sunday 03:00 UTC tick starts a
  fresh generation.
end note

note over Consumer,D1 #D9EAD3
  On a fresh install the liked and playlist exclusion sets
  are frozen first. The first eligible artist then becomes a
  usable, growing live shelf instead of making visitors wait
  for the entire multi-day crawl. Existing installations gain
  new eligible recordings with each validated song batch. Both generations'
  known filters and Never decisions apply in the same transaction.
  Final replacement keeps its original overlap baseline and gates.
  During provider waits, bounded database-only publication can expose
  already validated staging without Spotify calls or clearing retry gates.
end note

note over Consumer,D1 #D9EAD3
  Full 50-item Spotify pages use chunked multi-row INSERTs.
  Enrichment is capped at 30 tracks per delivery. Together
  these keep every unit below D1 Free's 50-query ceiling.
  Completed/skipped work rows are deleted immediately, so
  pending selection never scans a growing completed prefix.
end note

@enduml
